Experiment No. 10
Aim:

To use Wireshark and Cookies Editor to inspect cookies on a social media webpage and understand how to detect and prevent cookie-grabbing attacks.

Software Required:
Wireshark
Cookies Editor Extension
Google Chrome
Internet Connection
Theory:

Wireshark: A network analysis tool used to capture and examine network packets.

Cookies: Small pieces of data stored in a browser to remember user sessions and preferences.

Cookies Editor: A browser extension used to view and manage website cookies.

Cookie-Grabbing Attack: An attack in which hackers try to steal session cookies to gain unauthorized access to user accounts.

Procedure:

Part A: Using Wireshark

Open Wireshark.
Select the active network interface (Wi-Fi/Ethernet).
Click on Start Capture.
Open Chrome and visit a social media website.
Observe the captured network packets.
Apply the http or tls filter.
Stop the packet capture.

Part B: Using Cookies Editor

Open Google Chrome.
Install the Cookies Editor extension.
Visit a social media website.
Click on the Cookies Editor extension.
View the cookies associated with the website.
Observe cookie properties such as name, domain and security attributes.
Defensive Measures:
Always use HTTPS websites.
Enable the Secure attribute for cookies.
Use the HttpOnly attribute to prevent JavaScript access to cookies.
Enable the SameSite attribute.
Avoid using unsecured public Wi-Fi.
Enable Two-Factor Authentication.
Keep browsers updated.
Result:

Successfully studied Wireshark and Cookies Editor to inspect network traffic and website cookies. Also, understood the defensive techniques used to detect and prevent cookie-grabbing attacks.
